WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.459
When learning a language on your own for the
first time, it’s hard to know what path

00:00:03.459 --> 00:00:04.459
to follow.

00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:08.490
There are so many popular methods, strategies,
and learning pathways out there that most

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:12.690
people just pick the first method they find
and start learning.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:17.660
This works well for a while, but most people
soon find that the method they’re using

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:23.039
is too boring, too stressful, or simply too
abstract, and unable to help them use their

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:24.699
target language productively.

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:26.090
What if there were a better way?

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:29.830
What if you could find a tried-and-tested
method that is:

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.599
Well-organized, and structured according to
the major language skills?

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:36.620
Capable of helping you memorize words and
sentences easily and naturally?

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.579
Able to develop your ability to think directly
in your target language?

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:42.469
Built upon a foundation of stress-free feedback?

00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:43.710
Both fun and rewarding?

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.480
Fortunately, there’s no need to wonder.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.490
In this video, I’ll be sharing with you
some of the foundational concepts of that

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:51.490
method.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:55.090
And don’t forget to stay tuned till the
very end, because I’ve got a surprise for

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.300
you that you won’t want to miss!

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.609
Hello, I am Luca Lampariello, I am a language
coach who speaks 14 languages.

00:01:00.609 --> 00:01:05.850
On this channel, I help people learn foreign
languages more efficiently, faster, and in

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:06.850
a fun way.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:11.040
This video is part two in a series on how
to start learning a new language.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.570
By now, I’ll assume you have seen the first
video of the series and picked up a high-quality

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:20.230
beginner resource based on the recommendations
I made there.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:24.890
But if you haven’t seen that video yet,
go check it out now—I’ve included a link

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.570
in the description box.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:31.899
Once you have your beginner resource in hand,
you can start working on the one activity

00:01:31.899 --> 00:01:38.600
that will be the backbone of all of your future
language learning success—building skills!

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:44.810
This is an important detail that often gets
overlooked, because language learning is,

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:49.290
first and foremost, about acquiring skills.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:54.759
About learning how to read, write, speak,
and listen, along with all of the countless

00:01:54.759 --> 00:02:01.610
subskills that any competent language speaker
has in his or her toolbox.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:05.840
In traditional classroom settings, the development
of skills often gets overlooked in favor of

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.219
facts.

00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:11.910
Facts in the form of conjugation tables, word
lists, grammar explanations, the names of

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.080
verb tenses, and so on.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.420
All of these things are related to language,
but it’s important to realize that knowing

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:25.030
these things has nothing to do with speaking
and using a language productively.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:29.770
For example, did you ever consult a verb table
when learning your native language?

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.050
I don’t think so!

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.640
So my method, the method I’m going to be
talking to you about today, doesn’t waste

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.040
your time with such things.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.890
Instead, I’ll be teaching you how to use
translation to develop a core of authentic

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:44.050
language skills—the very skills that native
speakers of your target language use each

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:45.050
and every day!

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:46.050
Let’s dive in:

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:51.630
Step 1: Gain a Deep Understanding of the Content
In Part 1 of this series, I spoke at length

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:56.360
about the importance of bilingual texts, and
in particular, bilingual dialogues with accompanying

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.360
audio files.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.190
Now, if you’ve followed my advice, the resource
you’ve chosen contains many of these dialogues,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:05.540
which you’ll now be using to begin learning
your target language.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:10.750
In this first step, the goal is to learn how
to take these beginner dialogues—which should

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:16.550
come in both your native and target languages—and
use them to develop a deep understanding of

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:20.060
what the target language version of the dialogue
actually means.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:25.340
As an absolute beginner, the first skills
you’ll need to develop are listening and

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:26.340
reading.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.760
That way, you’ll be able to interpret the
sounds of your target language, and decipher

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:34.220
the written language used to represent those
sounds.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:39.440
Luckily, your bilingual dialogues should have
everything you need to start listening and

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:40.860
reading quickly.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:44.790
Once you get comfortable with following along
with the text and audio, you can develop a

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:49.260
deeper understanding of the content of the
dialogue in a number of ways.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.680
For example:
Read the target language text and listen to

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:58.650
the audio at the same time
Read the text in your target language only,

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:01.930
without audio
Read the text in your native language only

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:06.960
Read the text out loud in your target language
Compare both the target language and native

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.100
language versions of the dialogue, and use
the accompanying grammar notes to clarify

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.220
any details you did not originally understand.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:19.540
Completing these activities will help you
progressively gain a deeper and deeper understanding

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:24.360
of what any given dialogue in your coursebook
actually means.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:30.350
At this stage, focus solely on grasping what
the dialogue means—don’t worry about translation

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:33.660
or memorization yet.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.500
Those will come later.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.610
Step 2: Translate the Text into Your Native
Language

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:42.510
Now that you understand what the target language
version of your dialogue is supposed to mean,

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:47.490
we’re going to complete our first translation
step—taking the target language text and

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.420
translating it into your native language.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:54.590
Since your chosen beginner resource likely
comes with a native-language translation of

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:59.210
the dialogue already, you might be curious
why this is necessary.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:02.310
The answer is personalization.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:08.330
Your brain is more likely to memorize information
that it deems important or personally meaningful

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:09.330
to you.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.730
Straight out of your textbook, the dialogue
you’ve been working on probably doesn’t

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:15.180
have much personal relevance.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:19.890
Someone else wrote the dialogue, so it’s
not something that’s unique to you or your

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:20.890
circumstances.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:25.260
By taking the target language dialogue, and
using what you’ve learned so far to translate

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.990
that dialogue into your own words, you’re
doing two things:

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:33.930
Creating a unique, more personal, and more
memorable version of the dialogue

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:38.930
Paying deeper attention to the content and
structure of the original dialogue, so you

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:41.780
can translate it faithfully into your own
language.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:46.310
Together, these 2 steps will help further
solidify your knowledge of the dialogue, and

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:50.220
make it much easier to memorize the words
and phrases it contains.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.941
At this point, it’s important to consider
how you’ll perform this translation, and

00:05:53.941 --> 00:05:55.240
where you’ll keep it.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:00.889
For most people, I recommend either:
Writing the translation by hand into a dedicated

00:06:00.889 --> 00:06:04.660
language notebook
Typing the translation and saving it using

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:07.460
software like Google Docs, or Evernote.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:11.870
Personally, I enjoy typing my translations
into a Google Doc.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:18.950
Typing allows me to complete the process quite
quickly, and doing so in word-processing software

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:22.440
allows me to move and reformat things easily.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.870
Step 3: Translate the Text Back into Your
Target Language

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:27.250
Now for the final step.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:32.750
You’re going to take your personalized,
native language version of the original dialogue,

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:35.710
and translate it back into your target language.

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:41.700
This is the most powerful step of this entire
process, since it provides a number of key

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:44.330
benefits:
It tests your brain’s ability to recall

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:49.530
what it learned in the earlier two steps
It tests your ability to form phrases and

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:54.510
sentences in your target language
It compels you to think directly in your target

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:58.450
language, so that your final sentences convey
the correct meaning

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:02.410
So, where do we start, how do we actually
do this?

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:04.690
First we actually start by waiting.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:08.780
Once you’ve completed the native language
translation in the last step, it’s best

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:15.270
to wait anywhere from 24 to 48 hours before
translating your dialogue back into your target

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:16.270
language.

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.090
Why should you wait?

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:21.520
Because waiting helps you forget!

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:23.090
And why should you forget?

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:28.020
Because information that is forgotten and
then later recalled will actually create stronger

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:33.510
memories than information that was never forgotten
at all in the first place.

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.990
Traditional schooling teaches us that forgetting
is a bad thing, but neuroscience research

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:41.020
has shown that forgetting plays this fundamental
part in memorization.

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:45.860
To repeat, if we forget something and then
are reminded of what we’ve forgotten, we

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:48.660
will remember it better in the future.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:53.020
By waiting a day or two to complete your second
translation step, you’ll be taking advantage

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:56.400
of this important neural mechanism.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:01.340
Once you’re ready to translate back, here’s
what you should keep in mind:

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:06.400
Translate sentence by sentence
Read your entire native language version of

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.630
each sentence first, and then attempt to convey
the meaning of that sentence into your target

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:12.630
language.

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:14.680
Avoid translating word-by-word, if possible.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:19.970
If you find that you’ve forgotten a word
or phrase, try to wrack your brain for three

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:23.470
to ten seconds, and try hard to remember it.

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:28.419
If you still can’t remember after that time,
leave a blank space in your translation, and

00:08:28.419 --> 00:08:29.770
look it up later.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:35.370
Once you look them up, those forgotten words
and phrases will be more strongly remembered

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:36.700
going forward.

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:40.279
When you’ve finished this “reverse translation”
step to the best of your ability, you can

00:08:40.279 --> 00:08:47.029
compare your new target language version with
the original version in your coursebook.

00:08:47.029 --> 00:08:52.100
This step provides the “stress-free” feedback
I hinted at earlier, as it helps you observe

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:56.880
the kinds of mistakes you make in the language,
as well as the kinds of things you’re more

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.670
likely to both forget and remember.

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:01.670
So there you have it!

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:07.190
These are the three key steps that will help
you start learning any language through translation.

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:12.960
To recap, those steps are:
Gain a deep understanding of the content

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:20.040
Translate the text into your native language
Translate the text back into your target language

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:25.450
These steps are a distillation of the method
I’ve used to learn ten of the fourteen languages

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:27.180
I speak today.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:33.380
If you put them to use, and do so often, you’ll
quickly begin to read, understand, and think

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:35.770
in your target language—even as a beginner!

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:40.890
If you’d like to learn more about this method—including,
among other things, the powerful learning

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:47.600
plan that will help you rotate through these
activities consistently and efficiently—take

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.520
a look in the description box below.

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:54.500
I’ve left a link to my brand-new online
course, which will teach you everything you

00:09:54.500 --> 00:10:00.570
need to know about putting my Bidirectional
Translation to work for you, so you, too,

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:02.450
can learn any language!

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:03.740
As always, thanks for watching, and happy
language learning!

